From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 1 Sep 2026 18:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] user_manager: do not touch adb from userUnlocked

userUnlocked() is the reply to a binder call the container makes from
system_server's UserController thread. 1.4.2 calls adb_connect() there
unconditionally, and that is doubly wrong.

It raises RuntimeError whenever "adb start-server" fails, which is easy on a
device where adb has no writable HOME:

  RuntimeError: Command failed: % adb start-server

The exception escapes the callback, so the reply never comes, the thread stays
blocked, and sixty seconds later Android's watchdog kills system_server:

  Watchdog: at lineageos.waydroid.UserMonitor.userUnlocked(UserMonitor.java:81)
  Watchdog: at org.lineageos.platform.internal.WayDroidService.onUnlockUser
  Watchdog: *** GOODBYE!

Android restarts, unlocks the user, calls again and loops. On mindphone that
presented as a UI stuck on "phone is starting", processes climbing from 74 to
87 and SystemUI instances accumulating as each generation was orphaned - with
sys.boot_completed=1 the whole time, so "waydroid status" read healthy.

Suppressing the exception is not sufficient, which is worth stating plainly
because it was tried first: the call still blocks long enough to lose the race
with the watchdog. And when adb does connect, Android raises its "Allow USB
debugging?" dialog, which takes focus and leaves a second window that never
resolves into the full UI.

The app list sync sits immediately after this call, so launcher entries never
appeared either.

1.6.0 stopped auto-connecting adb on session start for these reasons and 1.6.3
gates the call on auto_adb. This does the same, defaulting off, so a machine
that wants it can still opt in through waydroid.cfg.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [fixed differently upstream in 1.6.3]
---
--- a/tools/services/user_manager.py
+++ b/tools/services/user_manager.py
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 import os
 import threading
 import tools.config
+from contextlib import suppress
 import tools.helpers.net
 from tools.interfaces import IUserMonitor
 from tools.interfaces import IPlatform
@@ -66,7 +67,33 @@
     def userUnlocked(uid):
         logging.info("Android with user {} is ready".format(uid))
 
-        tools.helpers.net.adb_connect(args)
+        # Do not touch adb from here unless explicitly asked to.
+        #
+        # userUnlocked() is the reply to a binder call the container makes from
+        # system_server's UserController thread. Anything slow or throwing here
+        # delays that reply, and after sixty seconds Android's own watchdog
+        # kills system_server:
+        #
+        #   Watchdog: at lineageos.waydroid.UserMonitor.userUnlocked
+        #   Watchdog: at ...WayDroidService.onUnlockUser
+        #   Watchdog: *** GOODBYE!
+        #
+        # Android restarts, unlocks the user, calls again and loops, leaving the
+        # UI stuck on "phone is starting" while orphaned SystemUI processes pile
+        # up. sys.boot_completed stays 1 throughout, so "waydroid status" looks
+        # healthy the whole time.
+        #
+        # adb_connect() is both slow and hazardous here: it raises RuntimeError
+        # when "adb start-server" fails, and when it succeeds it makes Android
+        # raise its "Allow USB debugging?" dialog, which takes focus and leaves
+        # a second window that never resolves to the full UI.
+        #
+        # Suppressing the exception is not enough - the call still blocks. 1.6.0
+        # stopped auto-connecting for these reasons and 1.6.3 gates it on
+        # auto_adb; this does the same, defaulting off.
+        if tools.config.load(args)["waydroid"].get("auto_adb", "False") == "True":
+            with suppress(RuntimeError):
+                tools.helpers.net.adb_connect(args)
 
         platformService = IPlatform.get_service(args)
         if platformService:
